description:
persona/src/main.rs:282-284 documents the command as:

    /// Fetches a JWT-SVID with the challenge embedded in the audience string, proving
    /// identity to a peer. The challenge becomes part of the JWT claims and signature,
    /// preventing replay attacks.

What the implementation actually does (main.rs:326-334) is:

    let audience_with_challenge = format!("{audience}?challenge={challenge}");
    client.fetch_jwtsvid(JwtsvidRequest { audience: vec![audience_with_challenge], spiffe_id: String::new() })

i.e. an ordinary FetchJWTSVID with a string appended to the audience. Three problems:

(1) No proof occurs. Attestor::prove() -- the trait method whose entire purpose is signing a challenge with the key material backing the claim (persona-attestors/src/lib.rs:117-129) -- is never called by this command or by the service it calls. The identity is asserted by the daemon ephemeral key, exactly as for `fetch-jwt`. The command is named and documented after a cryptographic operation it does not perform.

(2) "Preventing replay attacks" is not delivered. The challenge is not bound to a nonce claim and is never validated by anything; it survives only as a substring of the aud claim. Replay protection then depends on the verifier doing an exact string match on an audience value that includes the nonce -- a non-standard convention documented nowhere, and one that ValidateJWTSVID itself would only satisfy if the caller passed the identical concatenated string. A peer following the ordinary SPIFFE flow (match aud against its own audience URI) gets no replay protection at all, while the doc comment tells the developer it has some.

(3) The challenge is interpolated unescaped. Trigger: an audience that already contains a query string, e.g. `persona prove --audience "https://x.example?tenant=a" --challenge abc`, produces "https://x.example?tenant=a?challenge=abc". AudienceExtensions::parse (persona-core/src/audience.rs:121-124) splits on the first "?" only, so the parameter list becomes the single malformed token "tenant=a?challenge=abc". A challenge containing "&" similarly injects extra audience parameters.

What goes wrong: a developer reads the doc comment, uses `persona prove` as an authentication primitive against a peer, and ships something with none of the freshness or proof-of-possession properties the comment promises.

Fix direction: either implement it against Attestor::prove() and carry the assertion in a dedicated claim, or rename it and rewrite the doc comment to say plainly that it is fetch-jwt with the challenge appended to the audience, with a ponytail: marker naming the upgrade path. Percent-encode the challenge either way.
